    answer = []
    for index, color in queries:
        colors[index] = color
        same = 0
        for i in range(n - 1):
            if colors[i] != 0 and colors[i] == colors[i + 1]:
                same += 1
        answer.append(same)
    return answer
```
fail[speed]: Counting every pair again after each query puts a loop of n steps inside the loop over the queries: O(n × q). Only the painted cell's two neighbours can change the count, so keep the count and update it.
edges:
- one cell :: n == 1 :: color_the_array(1, [[0, 4]])
- painting the same cell again :: n > 1 and len(set([q[0] for q in queries])) < len(queries) :: color_the_array(2, [[0, 1], [1, 1], [0, 1]])
- a cell that matches two neighbours :: n > 2 and len(set([q[0] for q in queries])) == len(queries) and any(q[0] - 1 in [p[0] for p in queries[:i]] and q[0] + 1 in [p[0] for p in queries[:i]] for i, q in enumerate(queries)) :: color_the_array(3, [[0, 7], [2, 7], [1, 7]])
edge_count: 2
